p://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-22T22:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Conejos, CO; Rio Grande, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.81d377f57d32bcbd398ddd52adb2ac4bc44c6507.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.81d377f57d32bcbd398ddd52adb2ac4bc44c6507.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Warning","headline":"Flood Warning issued October 22 at 6:49PM MDT until October 25 at 6:00PM MDT by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"...The National Weather Service in Pueblo has issued a Flood Warning\nfor the following rivers in Colorado...\n\nRio Grande Near Del Norte affecting Rio Grande County.\n\nFor the Upper Rio Grande River...including Del Norte...Minor flooding\nis forecast.\n\n* WHAT...Minor flooding is forecast.\n\n* WHERE...Rio Grande  near Del Norte.\n\n* WHEN...From Friday morning to Sunday evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...At 6.0 feet, Minor Flood. Considerable agricultural\nflooding occurs.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 5:45 PM MDT Thursday the stage was 4.1 feet.\n- Bankfull stage is 5.0 feet.\n- Forecast...The river is expected to rise above flood stage\ntomorrow morning to a crest of 6.0 feet tomorrow evening. It will\nthen fall below flood stage Sunday morning.\n- Flood stage is 5.5 feet.\n- Flood History...This crest compares to a previous crest of 5.9\nfeet on 06/09/1985.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-25T18:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Rio Grande, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.153b5dda8f9373819ffabef65fe721dd445f03bd.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.153b5dda8f9373819ffabef65fe721dd445f03bd.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Warning","headline":"Flood Warning issued October 22 at 6:41PM MDT until October 23 at 3:45AM MDT by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Small stream flooding caused by excessive rainfall is\nexpected.\n\n* WHERE...Southern Mineral County in south central Colorado...\nWestern Rio Grande County in south central Colorado...\n\n* WHEN...Until 345 AM MDT Friday.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding of rivers, creeks, streams, and other low-lying\nand flood-prone locations is imminent or occurring. Rock falls are\noccuring in the steeper terrain.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 639 PM MDT, Doppler radar and automated rain gauges indicated\nheavy rain. Flooding is already occurring in the warned area.\nBetween 1 and 3.5 inches of rain have fallen.\n- Additional rainfall amounts up to 2 inches are possible in the\nwarned area.\n- Some locations that will experience flooding include...\nWolf Creek Pass and the creeks and streams flowing into the San\nJuan River.\n- Highway 160 over Wolf Creek Pass is closed due to flooding.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-23T03:45:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Mineral, CO; Rio Grande, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.5289a6353d07b40dfaad5f421c3d49793e9f3d0c.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.5289a6353d07b40dfaad5f421c3d49793e9f3d0c.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Warning","headline":"Flood Warning issued October 22 at 5:59PM MDT until October 22 at 10:15PM MDT by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"Heavy rainfall is causing flooding along the South Fork of the Rio\nGrande, which is forecast to crest at 9.2 feet around 1 AM tonight.\nIf you live near the South Fork of the Rio Grande, and the main stem\nof the Rio Grande, take precautions to protect life and property from\nflooding.\n\n* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues.\n\n* WHERE...Southwestern Saguache, Central Mineral and Western Rio\nGrande Counties...\n\n* WHEN...Until 1015 PM MDT.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding of rivers, creeks, streams, and other low-lying\nand flood-prone locations is imminent or occurring. Several\nstructures are flooded and some sand bagging operations are\ncontinuing. Streams continue to rise due to excess runoff from\ncontinued rainfall.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 552 PM MDT, South Fork Fire and Colorado State Patrol report\ncontinued rainfall across the warned area.\n- Water is currently near Highway 160 at Wolf Creek Ranch at mile\nmarker 177.  Several homes are also being flooded in the South\nFork area.  The river gauge on the south fork of the Rio Grande\n(SRGC2) is rapidly rising, and forecast to crest at 9.2 feet by 1\nAM tonight, and slowly fall during the overnight hours.  The main\nstem of the Rio Grande will likely run high down toward Del\nNorte.\n- Some locations that will experience flooding include...\nDel Norte, South Fork, Wagon Wheel Gap, Wolf Creek Pass and\nCreede.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-22T22:15:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Mineral, CO; Rio Grande, CO; Saguache, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.3d025e343077c60bfd8f01d7a0415054feb8704b.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.3d025e343077c60bfd8f01d7a0415054feb8704b.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued October 22 at 2:13PM MDT until October 23 at 6:00PM MDT by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...The eastern San Juan Mountains and Upper Rio Grande Valley.\n\n* WHEN...Through Friday afternoon.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Ample sub-tropcial moisture will bring periods of moderate to\nheavy rain across the area from tonight into Friday morning\nbefore winding down Friday afternoon.\n- The eastern San Juan Mountains and Upper Rio Grande Valley.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-23T18:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Upper Rio Grande Valley/Eastern San Juan Mountains Below 10000 Ft; Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.d926dc6d652870f5f8b98ab69b684b84f5ef12b5.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.d926dc6d652870f5f8b98ab69b6